From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=EC=8B=AC=EC=83=81=EC=9B=90?= <dev.chamomile@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:57:10 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] windows: the dirty-dump tests write in hole units, as a diff
 snapshot does

On Windows a diff snapshot writes 64 KiB units whole (`platform::fs::HOLE_UNIT`),
because NTFS allocates a sparse file in those units. A clean page in a dirty unit
is written with the data it has, which is the base's. The two upstream tests of
dump_dirty failed on Windows for two reasons.

test_dump_dirty counts in pages. With 4 KiB pages, its two-page regions are in
one unit, and the unit's clean page is written.

dump_dirty_correctness writes in a different order from a snapshot. It set the
file's length before the file was made sparse, which allocates the whole file on
NTFS: "8 blocks allocated, 0 expected". Vm::snapshot_memory_to_file calls
keep_holes before set_len. Its oracle also wrote single pages, and its slots were
any number of pages, where a VM's are MiB multiples.

Both tests now count in hole units. That is the host page on Linux and macOS, so
there they are upstream's tests unchanged except for the writer. Each file is
made sparse before its length is set, and is written through SparseWriter, as the
snapshot is. The property test's slots are whole units, and its oracle writes a
unit whole when one of its pages is dirty. A bitmap bit is still a page, and one
bit dirties its unit.

Measured on Windows Server 2022: vstate::memory and platform::fs pass, 28 of 28
(the two failed before with and without the lazy-mprotect change).
